// Generated from production/story/story-script.md
export const story={"version":2,"prologue":[{"speaker":"Sanjaya","text":"Two branches of one family have come to Kurukshetra prepared for war.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Sanjaya","text":"Arjun stands with the Pandavas, his four brothers. Their cousins stand across the field. Years of rivalry, a lost kingdom, exile and failed efforts at peace have brought them here.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Sanjaya","text":"Krishna, who sought peace between them, now guides Arjun's chariot.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Dhritarashtra","text":"Sanjaya, what happened when my sons and the sons of Pandu assembled at Kurukshetra, ready to fight?","bridge":false}],"chapters":[{"n":1,"title":"When the bow falls","sanskrit":"अर्जुनविषादयोग","subtitle":"The courage to admit confusion","mood":"dawn","scenes":[{"id":"1.1","title":"Between the armies","ref":21,"range":"1.2–27","source":"1.2–27. The commanders' catalogue is compressed; the chariot request follows 1.21–23.","beats":[{"speaker":"Sanjaya","text":"The commanders studied their opponents. Conches answered across the field. Then Arjun spoke.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"Take the chariot between the armies, Krishna. Let me see those who have come to fight us.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Look, Arjun. Here are the Kurus assembled.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Sanjaya","text":"There stood Bhishma, an elder of his family. Beside him was Drona, his teacher. Around them, Arjun recognised relatives, friends and men he had known for years.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Arjun’s conflict begins when an abstract duty becomes painfully personal. Understanding his grief matters before we try to understand Krishna’s answer.","example":"A difficult decision can look simple from a distance. When the people affected are people you love, the cost becomes real.","context":"","staging":["Wheels cross uneven ground. The camera travels with the chariot, not past it. We stop facing two distant figures."]},{"id":"1.2","title":"The hand that trembles","ref":28,"range":"1.28–31","source":"1.28–31.","beats":[{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"These are my own people.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"My strength is leaving me. My mouth is dry. I cannot keep my hand steady on the bow.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"I came here ready to fight. Now I see who stands before me, and I cannot see any good in killing them.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Feeling overwhelmed is part of Arjun’s story. His distress is taken seriously; admitting it opens the conversation.","example":"Before a hard conversation, notice your breathing and tension. Naming what you feel can help you explain what is happening instead of pretending to be fine.","context":"","staging":["Arjun's grip changes. His fingers strain, then loosen. Krishna remains present without filling the silence.","The armies remain visible, but our attention narrows to the small movement of his hand."]},{"id":"1.3","title":"What would victory leave?","ref":35,"range":"1.32–44","source":"1.32–44.","beats":[{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"What would I do with a kingdom won like this? The people for whom we might want it are standing there, risking their lives.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"They may be blinded by greed. Must we follow them into the same wrong?","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"When families are destroyed, what happens to those left behind? Who will sustain their lives, their customs, their remembrance of the dead?","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Sanjaya","text":"He feared the deaths before him, and the unravelling that might follow them.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Arjun is asking a moral question, not simply trying to escape danger: can a desired outcome justify its human cost?","example":"Before chasing a promotion or winning an argument, ask what your pursuit is doing to your relationships and integrity. The reward is only part of the decision.","context":"These are Arjun's arguments, including concerns shaped by the social and ritual order of the epic. The opening does not make every assumption in his speech Krishna's teaching. See the note on 1.40–44.","staging":[]},{"id":"1.4","title":"No answer yet","ref":47,"range":"1.45–47","source":"1.45–47.","beats":[{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"If they strike me while I am unarmed and do not resist, that seems better than this.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Sanjaya","text":"Overcome with grief, Arjun set aside his bow and arrows and sat down.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The first chapter ends without a neat answer. Recognising that your present way of thinking cannot resolve a problem can be the beginning of learning.","example":"You do not need to manufacture certainty. You can say, “This matters, and I need help thinking it through.”","context":"","staging":["Arjun lowers himself into the chariot. The bow rests beside him. Hold the shot long enough to notice that the confident posture from the entrance is gone.","Stay beside him. Chapter II begins in the same silence."]}],"understand":"Arjun's crisis joins grief, moral concern, attachment and uncertainty about responsibility. Krishna's teaching begins because Arjun cannot resolve it by himself.","reflection":"What makes a difficult choice hard for you: the act itself, its consequences, or whom it might hurt?"},{"n":2,"title":"Before you decide","sanskrit":"सांख्ययोग","subtitle":"Self, action and inner balance","mood":"gold","scenes":[{"id":"2.1","title":"Teach me","ref":7,"range":"2.1–10","source":"2.1–10.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Arjun, this collapse does not become you. Stand up and meet what is before you.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"How can I raise a weapon against Bhishma and Drona? They deserve my respect. I do not know whether winning or losing would be worse.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"I can no longer tell what my duty asks of me. Tell me what is truly good. I am asking to learn from you.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Sanjaya","text":"He said he would not fight. Then he fell silent between the armies.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Arjun shifts from defending a conclusion to asking for guidance. Humility here means being willing to examine your understanding.","example":"When a recurring problem defeats you, ask someone thoughtful to challenge your assumptions, not merely agree with your preferred answer.","context":"","staging":["Arjun looks toward Krishna instead of the opposing army."]},{"id":"2.2","title":"What changes, what endures","ref":20,"range":"2.11–30","source":"2.11–30.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"You speak with concern, yet your grief has not seen the whole truth. You, I, and these rulers have not simply come out of nothing, nor will we cease altogether.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"The embodied self passes through childhood, youth and age, and passes onward when a body dies. The self is not born with the body and is not destroyed with it.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Pleasure and pain arrive and pass. Learn to endure their changes without losing sight of what endures.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Krishna teaches the enduring self, or atman. This is a spiritual claim about our nature, not a claim that physical pain or another person’s life is unimportant.","example":"Your age, appearance and roles change. Reflect on the continuity of the one who experiences those changes; this offers an entry into the question of the self.","context":"","staging":["Light crosses Arjun's hand. Fleeting changes of scale and texture suggest age without introducing a literal memory sequence."]},{"id":"2.3","title":"Your action and its fruit","ref":47,"range":"2.31–53","source":"2.31–53.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Consider also the responsibility before you as a warrior. In this struggle, withdrawal does not by itself make you free of duty.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Give yourself to the action that is yours. Do not make its reward the reason for everything you do. And do not cling to doing nothing.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Practise steadiness in success and failure. Let understanding guide the work, rather than the demand that its outcome belong to you.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Non-attachment does not mean careless work. Choose and perform your action responsibly, then recognise that the outcome depends on more than your effort alone.","example":"Prepare thoroughly for an exam or interview. Your preparation is your responsibility; the final decision is not entirely yours. Learn from it without making it your whole identity.","context":"","staging":["His hand opens over the reins. The bow stays where Arjun placed it; the story does not resolve his conflict prematurely."]},{"id":"2.4","title":"A steadier person","ref":70,"range":"2.54–72","source":"2.54–72.","beats":[{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"How does someone with that steadiness live? How would I recognise it?","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Such a person is less driven by craving, less shaken by suffering, less carried away by pleasure. The senses can be gathered back, as a tortoise draws in its limbs.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Dwelling on an object can become attachment; frustrated desire can become anger, and anger can cloud discernment. Learn to see that movement before it carries you away.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Waters enter the ocean, yet it remains full and steady. Peace belongs to one who can receive experience without being ruled by every desire it brings.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Steadiness grows by understanding the chain of reaction. You still encounter pleasure and pain; they need not decide every action for you.","example":"When a message irritates you, pause before replying. Notice the story and demand building around it. A little space can prevent a temporary feeling from choosing your response.","context":"","staging":["A quiet ocean appears beyond the rail as a teaching image."]}],"understand":"Atman means the self in this teaching, which Krishna describes as enduring beyond bodily death. Non-attachment concerns the grasping relationship to results; it does not erase responsibility for how we act. His call to fight addresses Arjun's specific place in the epic.","reflection":"Where could you give an action your full care while loosening your demand for a particular reward?"},{"n":3,"title":"The work before you","sanskrit":"कर्मयोग","subtitle":"Action as a spiritual practice","mood":"earth","scenes":[{"id":"3.1","title":"Can I do nothing?","ref":5,"range":"3.1–9","source":"3.1–9.","beats":[{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"If understanding is so important, why direct me toward this terrible action? Your words seem to lead in different directions. Show me a clear way.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Both the discipline of knowledge and the discipline of action have their place. But freedom does not come simply from leaving work undone.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"No one remains entirely without action. Restraining the body while the mind clings to its desires is not freedom. Begin with the work before you, and change how you undertake it.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Karma yoga is a way of acting. Simply withdrawing from visible work does not remove desire or responsibility.","example":"Avoiding a difficult email is still a choice with consequences. Ask what responsible action is required, then do it without endless rehearsal of praise or blame.","context":"","staging":["The horses breathe. Cloth shifts. Even the apparently still scene is alive with movement."]},{"id":"3.2","title":"A world of giving","ref":13,"range":"3.10–19","source":"3.10–19.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Life is sustained through giving and receiving. Beings depend on food, food on rain, and the order of offering joins human action to a world larger than the individual.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"One who takes everything for themselves forgets that dependence. Do necessary work without holding it only for your own gain.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Yajna means sacrifice or offering. Here it widens the purpose of work beyond taking for yourself.","example":"When you eat, remember the people and natural processes behind the meal. Express that awareness through less waste, fair dealing and some contribution to others.","context":"","staging":["The turning form of a chariot wheel opens into a restrained image of rain, grain and hands offering food."]},{"id":"3.3","title":"Others are watching","ref":21,"range":"3.20–35","source":"3.20–35.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"What a respected person does becomes an example. Action can help sustain the world.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"There is nothing I need to gain, yet I continue to act. If I abandoned action, others would follow, and the order supporting their lives would suffer.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Offer your work to me. Let go of possessiveness and the fever of expectation. Attend to your own responsibility, even when another person's path looks easier.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Lokasangraha is the sustaining of the world. Mature action considers the example it sets and the people it affects.","example":"A team leader who admits mistakes makes honesty easier for everyone. Your everyday conduct teaches something even when you are not giving advice.","context":"","staging":["Arjun sees people behind his own ranks, not only the opponents ahead."]},{"id":"3.4","title":"What drives us against our judgment?","ref":37,"range":"3.36–43","source":"3.36–43.","beats":[{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"What makes a person do wrong even when they do not want to—as though something forces them?","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Desire, and the anger that grows from it. Like smoke covering fire, it can obscure knowledge.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Begin with the senses. Strengthen discernment. Recognise that you are not exhausted by the impulses moving through you, and learn to meet this force without surrendering to it.","bridge":false}],"simple":"A craving is not an instruction. Notice it early, before it gathers the force of a complete justification.","example":"If you repeatedly make purchases you regret, create a pause before paying. Use that space to ask what feeling the purchase is meant to satisfy.","context":"","staging":["The smoke becomes the flame of an offering. Chapter IV asks what understanding brings to action."]}],"understand":"Karma means action; karma yoga is a discipline of acting without possessive attachment to its fruits. Yajna begins with sacrifice or offering and extends through the chapter's account of mutual dependence.","reflection":"Which action in your day supports people whose contribution you rarely notice?"},{"n":4,"title":"Wisdom in action","sanskrit":"ज्ञानकर्मसंन्यासयोग","subtitle":"Discernment, offering and renewal","mood":"gold","scenes":[{"id":"4.1","title":"The teacher and time","ref":7,"range":"4.1–10","source":"4.1–10.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"This yoga has been taught before, handed onward through generations. As it became obscured, I am teaching it to you again, because you are my devotee and friend.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"You speak of teaching in an age long before your birth. How am I to understand that?","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"You and I have passed through many births. I know them; you do not. Though unborn and imperishable, I manifest through my own power. When dharma declines, I appear to protect the good, overcome wrongdoing and restore it.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Krishna describes divine incarnation and the renewal of dharma—the order and responsibility that sustain life. This is a central theological teaching of the Gita.","example":"In a smaller human setting, values survive when people practise and renew them. This is an analogy for renewal, not an equivalent of divine incarnation.","context":"","staging":[]},{"id":"4.2","title":"Inside the action","ref":18,"range":"4.11–23","source":"4.11–23.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Action is subtle. A motionless body can conceal a mind full of grasping. Work can be undertaken without the claim that everything is mine.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"When craving for reward falls away, when the act is offered and guided by knowledge, work need not bind in the same way. Learn to see more than the body's movement.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Freedom is not measured only by how busy or quiet you look. The motive and understanding behind action matter.","example":"Two people can volunteer for the same work: one mainly seeks recognition, another seeks to help. The task is identical, but the inner relationship to it differs.","context":"","staging":["The offering flame reveals hands doing necessary work. The images remain openly symbolic."]},{"id":"4.3","title":"Ways to offer","ref":28,"range":"4.24–33","source":"4.24–33.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"People make offerings in many ways: through material gifts, restraint, study, disciplined breath and knowledge. These practices can purify the one who undertakes them.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"In the deepest vision, the offering, the act of offering and its destination belong to Brahman. Knowledge brings the meaning of action into view.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Spiritual practice can involve your possessions, attention, habits and understanding. Ritual is one expression within a wider discipline.","example":"Give a conversation your undivided attention. Relinquishing the urge to interrupt or check your phone can make a small practice of offering.","context":"","staging":["Separate shapes in the flame become a single continuous field of light, then settle back into the small fire."]},{"id":"4.4","title":"Ask and learn","ref":34,"range":"4.34–42","source":"4.34–42.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Approach those who see clearly with humility, sincere questions and a willingness to learn through service.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Knowledge can carry you across wrongdoing and confusion. Disciplined trust and steady practice matter. Cut through the doubt born of ignorance; establish yourself in yoga, and rise.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The Gita makes room for inquiry. Learning involves questions, practice and a willingness to change how you live.","example":"When learning a skill, find a capable teacher, ask precise questions and practise what you learn. Collecting quotations alone does not produce understanding.","context":"","staging":[]}],"understand":"Dharma includes sustaining order, right conduct and situated responsibility; no single English word covers it. Brahman is ultimate reality. The verse about four social orders at 4.13 is accessible in the source and addressed in the context notes; it is not silently rewritten as a modern career system.","reflection":"What question would you need to ask honestly before you could take your next step?"},{"n":5,"title":"A different kind of freedom","sanskrit":"कर्मसंन्यासयोग","subtitle":"Renunciation without indifference","mood":"green","scenes":[{"id":"5.1","title":"Must I leave?","ref":2,"range":"5.1–6","source":"5.1–6.","beats":[{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"You praise renunciation, and you also praise action. Which should I follow?","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Both can lead to the highest good, but the discipline of action is the more practicable path here. A true renouncer is not consumed by attraction and aversion.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Wisdom and disciplined action need not lead to different destinations. Learn what must be relinquished within the life you are living.","bridge":false}],"simple":"You need not wait for a different life to practise. Begin by changing your relationship to desire, aversion and the work before you.","example":"A new job or city may help, but it will not automatically remove resentment. You can begin examining that pattern where you are.","context":"","staging":[]},{"id":"5.2","title":"The lotus leaf","ref":10,"range":"5.7–12","source":"5.7–12.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Act with body, mind and understanding, giving up possessive attachment. Offer the action to the divine. Like water on a lotus leaf, it need not cling to you.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Clinging to the fruit binds. Releasing that clinging brings peace.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The lotus is a metaphor for inward freedom amid activity. It does not ask you to become emotionally unavailable.","example":"Care deeply for a project while allowing colleagues to contribute and decisions to change. Commitment does not require ownership of every detail.","context":"","staging":["A lotus leaf forms within the teaching landscape. Water gathers and rolls away. It is a visual metaphor, not an object Krishna has conjured into the historical battlefield."]},{"id":"5.3","title":"The same spiritual presence","ref":18,"range":"5.13–21","source":"5.13–21.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"The wise see with equality in a learned person, a cow, an elephant, a dog and a person whom society treats as an outcast.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Their forms and circumstances differ. Wisdom is not trapped by those differences. When understanding rests in the divine, outward pleasure no longer has to carry the whole weight of happiness.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Equal vision recognises spiritual dignity across status and species. It need not pretend that everyone has identical needs or abilities.","example":"Notice whether you change your basic respect according to someone’s title. Practise the same courtesy with a cleaner that you offer a director.","context":"","staging":["Each is given equal space and attention; none is an exotic decorative figure."]},{"id":"5.4","title":"Peace and friendship","ref":29,"range":"5.22–29","source":"5.22–29.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Pleasures born from contact have beginnings and endings. Learn to bear desire and anger without becoming their servant.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Those who turn inward, who work for the welfare of beings, who know me as the receiver of sacrifice, the lord of worlds and the friend of all beings, find peace.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Krishna joins self-discipline with a devotional vision of the divine as friend of all. Peace involves both inner freedom and relationship.","example":"Enjoy a good meal or a compliment, but notice when enjoyment becomes a demand for the feeling to continue. You can appreciate something without requiring it to last forever.","context":"","staging":["We return to Arjun's restless hand. He has heard what steadiness means. He must now ask how to practise it."]}],"understand":"Renunciation here is not simply geographical withdrawal. The chapter joins disciplined action, equal vision and knowledge of Krishna. Its spiritual goal reaches beyond feeling comfortable in daily life.","reflection":"Where do status or appearance decide how much attention you give another person?"},{"n":6,"title":"Return to the mind","sanskrit":"ध्यानयोग","subtitle":"Practice, balance and returning","mood":"blue","scenes":[{"id":"6.1","title":"Friend or opponent","ref":5,"range":"6.1–9","source":"6.1–9.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"One who does necessary work without depending on its reward is practising renunciation and yoga.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Raise yourself through your own discipline. The mind can become your ally; left ungoverned, it can work against you. Steadiness grows as it becomes less captive to pleasure, pain, praise and contempt.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The mind develops habits. Patient training can change your relationship to those habits; harsh self-judgment often adds another struggle.","example":"When you lose focus, notice it without insulting yourself. Return to the next small action. Repeated returns are part of the training.","context":"","staging":["Arjun's hand rests against the rail. He remains seated in the encounter, not transported to an unrelated meditation tutorial."]},{"id":"6.2","title":"A life that can support practice","ref":17,"range":"6.10–23","source":"6.10–23.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Find a suitable place, sit steadily and gather your attention. Let the mind turn toward me.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"This yoga is not helped by eating too much or too little, by sleeping too much or keeping yourself always awake. Bring balance to food, rest, activity and effort.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"A steady mind is like that flame. Through practice it can discover a joy that does not depend on the senses.","bridge":false}],"simple":"A sustainable rhythm supports meditation. Care for daily life is part of the practice, not a distraction from it.","example":"Start with a brief, regular period of quiet sitting. An achievable practice with adequate rest is more sustainable than an intense routine you cannot maintain.","context":"","staging":["A lamp appears in the teaching space. For a moment its flame is sheltered from the wind."]},{"id":"6.3","title":"Like trying to hold the wind","ref":35,"range":"6.24–36","source":"6.24–36.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Whenever the mind wanders, bring it back. Learn to see yourself in other beings and other beings in yourself, and to regard their joy and suffering with the seriousness of your own.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"But the mind is restless, forceful and stubborn. Controlling it feels like trying to hold the wind.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"It is difficult. Yet practice and the loosening of attachment can train it.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Meditation does not require never having a thought. Notice wandering, release the grip of the thought and gently return.","example":"While listening to someone, you may start planning your reply. Notice that movement and return to what the person is actually saying.","context":"","staging":["The flame moves. Its movement is allowed to be part of the scene; steadiness is not presented as instant perfection."]},{"id":"6.4","title":"What if I fall short?","ref":40,"range":"6.37–47","source":"6.37–47.","beats":[{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"What happens to someone who begins with faith but cannot complete the path? Are they lost, belonging nowhere?","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"No sincere effort toward the good is destroyed. Such practice bears onward, even through further births, and draws the seeker again toward understanding.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Continue. Among yogis, I hold especially close the one whose inner life rests in me, who worships with faith and love.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Krishna answers through the spiritual horizon of rebirth and devotion. The practical encouragement is to keep going rather than treating imperfection as total failure.","example":"Missing a week of practice does not erase what you learned. Resume at the next opportunity instead of waiting for a perfect restart.","context":"","staging":[]}],"understand":"Yoga means a spiritual discipline and the integration or steadiness it seeks; it is not limited to bodily postures. Krishna's reassurance includes rebirth, rather than merely promising that a useful habit will return tomorrow.","reflection":"What small act of returning could you practise without demanding immediate mastery?"},{"n":7,"title":"The world held together","sanskrit":"ज्ञानविज्ञानयोग","subtitle":"Knowing the source of life","mood":"green","scenes":[{"id":"7.1","title":"A thread through everything","ref":7,"range":"7.1–7","source":"7.1–7.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Turn your mind toward me and practise with me as your refuge. Hear how you may come to know me more fully.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Earth, water, fire, air, space, mind, understanding and the sense of \"I\" belong to my lower nature. Beyond this is the living principle that sustains the world.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Everything rests upon me, as beads are held upon a thread.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Krishna describes the divine as the underlying source and support of material and living existence. The thread connects what first appears separate.","example":"A necklace draws attention to its beads, but the thread makes it a necklace. Use the image to reflect on what sustains the many parts of your life.","context":"","staging":["Separate beads appear along a fine thread of light; the thread continues beyond the frame."]},{"id":"7.2","title":"The taste of water","ref":8,"range":"7.8–12","source":"7.8–12.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"I am the taste in water, the light in the sun and moon, the sacred sound in the Vedas. I am the fragrance of earth and the life in living beings.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"I am strength free from craving and attachment. The qualities through which nature appears arise from me, but do not contain the whole of me.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The teaching invites attention to familiar experiences as expressions of divine presence. The sacred need not begin with something spectacular.","example":"Drink a glass of water slowly. Notice the simple fact that it sustains you. Let gratitude interrupt the habit of taking ordinary things for granted.","context":"","staging":["A small bowl of water catches daylight within the symbolic landscape. Let this be simpler than the preceding vision."]},{"id":"7.3","title":"What obscures the source","ref":14,"range":"7.13–19","source":"7.13–19.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Caught in the qualities of nature, people fail to recognise what lies beyond them. This power of appearance, maya, is difficult to cross. Those who take refuge in me can cross it.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"People turn toward me in suffering, in search of knowledge, in hope of gain, or through wisdom. Each such turning matters. The wise devotee comes to recognise that all rests in the divine.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Maya concerns the power through which changing appearances and attachments can obscure reality. Krishna’s answer combines understanding with devotion.","example":"When frightened, you may notice only evidence that confirms your fear. Recognising that narrowing of vision can help you become more open to what is actually there.","context":"","staging":[]},{"id":"7.4","title":"Beyond a passing reward","ref":23,"range":"7.20–30","source":"7.20–30.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Desire often directs worship toward a particular reward. Such rewards have an end.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"My reality is not exhausted by what you can presently see. Attraction and aversion cloud understanding. Freed from their hold, steadfast seekers turn toward me and seek release from age and death.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The chapter moves from asking the divine for things toward seeking the divine itself. Temporary rewards cannot provide final fulfilment.","example":"It is natural to seek help with a problem. Also ask whether solving that one problem will address the deeper restlessness that keeps finding a new object.","context":"","staging":["The bowl's reflected light reaches the horizon. Arjun's next question concerns what lies beyond a lifetime."]}],"understand":"Maya names a divine power through which changing appearances can obscure their source. The chapter does not simply claim that everyday life is fake. Its movement is from limited desires toward knowing Krishna.","reflection":"Which ordinary experience might you meet with greater attention or gratitude?"},{"n":8,"title":"What endures","sanskrit":"अक्षरब्रह्मयोग","subtitle":"Remembrance, death and the imperishable","mood":"blue","scenes":[{"id":"8.1","title":"Name what you mean","ref":3,"range":"8.1–4","source":"8.1–4.","beats":[{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"What do you mean by Brahman, by the self, and by karma? How are you present in the embodied world—and how can you be known at death?","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Brahman is the imperishable, supreme reality. Adhyatma concerns one's inner spiritual nature. Karma here names the creative action through which embodied beings arise.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"The changing world is perishable. There is also the conscious governing principle, and I am present in the body as the lord of sacrifice.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The Gita uses some words in precise, context-dependent ways. Brahman points to imperishable reality; adhyatma turns inquiry toward the self.","example":"When a discussion becomes confusing, clarify the central words first. Shared definitions can reveal whether the disagreement is about the idea or the language.","context":"","staging":[]},{"id":"8.2","title":"Remember, and act","ref":7,"range":"8.5–10","source":"8.5–10.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"The direction of consciousness at death matters. What a person has repeatedly held in mind shapes that final orientation.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Therefore remember me at all times, and fulfil the action before you. Offer mind and understanding to me. Such remembrance grows through practice.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Krishna connects remembrance at death with the orientation of an entire life. Devotion and responsible action are meant to develop together.","example":"What you repeatedly return to shapes your attention. Make some room each day for the values and spiritual commitments you hope will guide you under pressure.","context":"","staging":["Arjun's bow is briefly visible beneath the widening sky. Contemplation has not erased the circumstance in which he must act."]},{"id":"8.3","title":"Beyond the long cycles","ref":20,"range":"8.11–22","source":"8.11–22.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Seekers discipline the senses and mind, remembering the imperishable and the sacred sound Om.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Worlds appear and withdraw through vast spans of time. Beyond these changes is an eternal reality that does not perish when beings perish. That is the highest destination, approached through undivided devotion.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The chapter describes cosmic time and liberation beyond repeated birth and death. It asks us to distinguish what changes from the imperishable.","example":"A calendar helps plan a week, but a larger view can change what you consider urgent. The cosmic scale here invites an even deeper reconsideration of your aims.","context":"","staging":["The imagined sky moves through immense cycles of arising and dissolution. The chariot remains a steady foreground reference."]},{"id":"8.4","title":"The practice is now","ref":27,"range":"8.23–28","source":"8.23–28.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"The teaching describes a bright path from which there is no return, and another by which the traveller returns. Knowing these paths, the yogi need not be bewildered.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Be established in yoga at all times. This understanding leads beyond the limited rewards of ritual, study, austerity and giving, toward the supreme origin.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The chapter’s cosmology is part of its religious teaching. Its immediate invitation is to practise steadily in the life you are living.","example":"Rather than postponing what matters until retirement or a crisis, make one modest, repeatable commitment today.","context":"","staging":[]}],"understand":"The chapter teaches remembrance, rebirth, cosmic cycles and liberation. Its paths after death are part of that religious account. A modern analogy about attention cannot replace those claims. Moksha means liberation from spiritual bondage and repeated birth and death.","reflection":"What do you repeatedly return to, and is it what you want to guide your life?"},{"n":9,"title":"A small offering","sanskrit":"राजविद्याराजगुह्ययोग","subtitle":"The intimacy of devotion","mood":"gold","scenes":[{"id":"9.1","title":"Present, and beyond","ref":4,"range":"9.1–10","source":"9.1–10.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"I will tell you a profound knowledge, joined with understanding that can be lived. Receive it with an open mind.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"My unmanifest presence pervades the world. Beings depend on me, yet I am not confined within them. Nature brings forth the moving and unmoving under my oversight.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Krishna holds together divine presence and transcendence: the world depends on the divine, while the divine is not limited to the world.","example":"A source can express itself in many forms without being exhausted by one form. This is a limited analogy to help approach a much larger theological claim.","context":"","staging":["The universe from the last chapter becomes a reflection in the small bowl. The source exceeds either image."]},{"id":"9.2","title":"What are we seeking?","ref":22,"range":"9.11–25","source":"9.11–25.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Some see only my human appearance and miss my deeper nature. Others turn toward me in steadfast devotion, remembering and worshipping.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Even the joys of heaven pass when the merit supporting them is spent. Those whose attention rests wholly in me seek a different destination. I attend to what sustains that devoted life.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Devotion is a reorientation of trust and purpose. This verse should not be treated as a guarantee of material success or protection from every difficulty.","example":"A durable commitment can steady you through uncertain circumstances. Practise it because it matters, without turning it into a transaction for guaranteed rewards.","context":"The promise of divine care in 9.22 is part of a devotional relationship. It is not a promise that worship will prevent every loss or produce financial success.","staging":[]},{"id":"9.3","title":"A leaf is enough","ref":26,"range":"9.26–29","source":"9.26–29.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"A leaf, a flower, fruit, or water—offered with devotion, I receive it.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Whatever you do, whatever you eat, whatever you give, whatever discipline you undertake: offer it to me. Let action become an offering, released from its binding claim on you.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"I am equal toward all beings. Those who love me dwell in me, and I in them.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Devotion is accessible through ordinary acts. Wealth and ceremony are not prerequisites for a sincere offering.","example":"Prepare a simple meal with gratitude, or do a necessary task with care. You do not need an impressive gesture to practise sincerity.","context":"","staging":["One leaf, softly lit. Arjun and Krishna remain present behind it. Resist the temptation to make every teaching visually immense."]},{"id":"9.4","title":"The possibility of turning","ref":30,"range":"9.30–34","source":"9.30–34.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Even a person whose conduct has been deeply wrong can turn toward me with undivided devotion and move toward righteousness. My devotee is not lost.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Sanjaya","text":"Krishna speaks of refuge reaching across the social divisions named in this teaching. Its invitation also reaches those whom that world placed at a disadvantage.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Turn your mind toward me. Give me your devotion and reverence. Make me your highest aim, and you will come to me.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Hope for transformation does not erase accountability. Genuine change must become visible in how someone lives and treats others.","example":"An apology becomes meaningful through repair and changed conduct. Do not confuse a declaration of change with the patient work of becoming different.","context":"","staging":[]}],"understand":"Bhakti is loving devotion to the divine. The text's hope for transformation does not excuse continued harm. Verse 9.32 also uses language of birth, gender and status that needs honest context; read the note rather than treating it as a modern declaration about social equality.","reflection":"What ordinary act could become an offering of sincere attention?"},{"n":10,"title":"Glimpses of the infinite","sanskrit":"विभूतियोग","subtitle":"Recognising divine expressions","mood":"gold","scenes":[{"id":"10.1","title":"A deepening relationship","ref":8,"range":"10.1–11","source":"10.1–11.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Hear more, Arjun. I speak for your good. I am the source from which everything proceeds.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Those who recognise this turn toward me with love. Remembering me together, they find joy. To those devoted in love, I give the discernment that leads them toward me; I dispel the darkness of ignorance with the light of knowledge.","bridge":false}],"simple":"In the Gita, knowledge and devotion can support each other. Understanding the divine source reshapes the heart’s orientation.","example":"What you understand deeply often changes what you love and how you act. Let reflection and practice inform each other.","context":"","staging":["Warmth returns to the space between teacher and listener."]},{"id":"10.2","title":"Help me remember","ref":20,"range":"10.12–23","source":"10.12–23.","beats":[{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"I accept what you tell me. You alone know yourself fully. Tell me the expressions of your presence through which I can keep remembering you.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"I am the self within the hearts of beings, their beginning, middle and end. Among lights, see the radiance of the sun; among the senses, the mind; in living beings, consciousness.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Krishna offers points of contemplation, not a catalogue that contains the whole divine. Familiar experiences can become reminders.","example":"Choose a recurring moment—the first daylight, a breath before work—to remember what gives your life direction.","context":"","staging":[]},{"id":"10.3","title":"The world becomes a reminder","ref":32,"range":"10.24–38","source":"10.24–38.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Recognise my expressions in the Himalayas, in the sacred syllable, in the peepal tree, in the Ganga, in the lion's strength.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"I am also time, wisdom, memory and the powers by which life unfolds. These are ways to turn your attention toward me.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Vibhuti means a manifestation of divine excellence or power. Admiration becomes contemplation rather than a demand to possess.","example":"When another person excels, try appreciation before comparison. Their ability can inspire you without becoming evidence that you are inadequate.","context":"","staging":["Himalayas, a peepal tree, a river and a lion appear as successive teaching images, connected by light. They are contemplative examples, not stops on a geographical chariot journey."]},{"id":"10.4","title":"Beyond the list","ref":42,"range":"10.39–42","source":"10.39–42. The last line is an authored bridge into Arjun's request in 11.3–4.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"There is no end to these manifestations. Whatever has splendour, beauty or power arises from a portion of my radiance.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"But why multiply examples? With one portion of myself, I sustain this entire universe.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"I have heard you describe it. Can I see?","bridge":true}],"simple":"The list ends by pointing beyond lists. Any particular image offers a glimpse of a reality greater than the image.","example":"A beautiful view can arrest your attention. Let the experience open curiosity and gratitude rather than feeling you must capture every detail.","context":"","staging":["The last image becomes too expansive to fit within its frame. We return to Krishna's near, familiar hand."]}],"understand":"Vibhuti means a manifestation of divine splendour or power. The examples direct contemplation; no list or image can contain the divine they indicate.","reflection":"Can admiration lead you toward gratitude without becoming a desire to possess?"},{"n":11,"title":"More than the eyes can hold","sanskrit":"विश्वरूपदर्शनयोग","subtitle":"Wonder, time and the universal form","mood":"cosmic","scenes":[{"id":"11.1","title":"The gift of sight","ref":8,"range":"11.1–14","source":"11.1–14.","beats":[{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"What you have taught me has lifted much of my confusion. I have heard how beings arise and pass away. I want to behold your sovereign form. If you think I can bear it, show me.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Behold my forms in their countless colours and kinds. See the whole world gathered here, and wonders you have never seen.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Your ordinary eyes cannot take in this vision. I grant you divine sight.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Sanjaya","text":"Then Krishna revealed the universal form. Its radiance was beyond the light the field had known.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The Vishvarupa is a divine revelation within the story. Our artwork is a symbolic interpretation, not an exact representation of that vision.","example":"Sometimes new understanding requires a different way of looking, not simply more of the same information. This is an analogy for perspective, not divine sight.","context":"","staging":["Krishna's familiar outline is still near. The horizon begins to open around it."]},{"id":"11.2","title":"Wonder becomes fear","ref":16,"range":"11.15–31","source":"11.15–31.","beats":[{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"I see beings and divine powers within you. I cannot find your beginning, your middle or your end. Your radiance fills every direction.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"And I see our warriors entering destruction. Bhishma, Drona, the sons of Dhritarashtra—rushing toward it like rivers toward the sea.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"I cannot find peace. Tell me who you are in this terrible form.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The universal form contains both creation and destruction. Arjun’s fear is part of the revelation, not a failure to appreciate it.","example":"Looking honestly at nature can reveal beauty and vulnerability together. Maturity makes room for both without demanding that reality contain only what comforts us.","context":"","staging":["Many faceless forms, arms and crowns unfold through immense space. Worlds seem held within worlds. The near rail stays legible as Arjun bends in reverence. Gradually, the vision includes dissolution. Figures of warriors move into an overwhelming blaze; no graphic injury.","His hand reaches for the rail. The awe has become difficult to bear."]},{"id":"11.3","title":"Time","ref":32,"range":"11.32–34","source":"11.32–34.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"I am Time, bringing worlds to their end. The warriors arrayed here will not remain, even without you.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Rise, Arjun. Become an instrument. These warriors stand already within that ending. Meet the battle before you.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Krishna places Arjun’s agency within a cosmic vision of time. The passage belongs to the epic’s specific moral and theological setting.","example":"You cannot stop every change around you. You can still examine your responsibility within change rather than claiming either total control or no responsibility at all.","context":"This difficult command belongs to Krishna's revelation and Arjun's particular role in the epic. It is not permission to identify one's own enemies with those warriors or to use religion to excuse violence.","staging":["Hold. Let the line land before another transition. The teaching image presents destruction as a dimension of the vision, not an exciting reward for the reader."]},{"id":"11.4","title":"The friend within the vastness","ref":54,"range":"11.35–55","source":"11.35–55.","beats":[{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"I have called you my friend and spoken lightly, not knowing the greatness before me. Forgive what I failed to understand.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"I am filled with wonder, and shaken by fear. Show me again a form I can bear: crowned, with mace and discus, with four arms.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Let this fear subside. See again the form you have asked for.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"Seeing your gentle human form, I can gather myself again.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Through undivided devotion I can be truly known, seen and entered. Work for me. Make me your highest aim. Be free of clinging and of hostility toward beings.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The immense vision returns to relationship and a way of living. Awe must find expression in devotion, action and freedom from enmity.","example":"After a powerful experience, ask how it changes your conduct tomorrow. Its significance need not depend on recreating the same intensity.","context":"","staging":["The universal vision draws inward. A restrained four-armed, crowned divine form bridges the transition. Then Krishna is once more the familiar figure on the chariot. Arjun's grip eases."]}],"understand":"Vishvarupa is the universal form. The revelation contains radiance and terror, creation and destruction. The scene returns to relationship and conduct; seeing it is not the end of Arjun's learning.","reflection":"When an experience changes your perspective, how might that change become visible in your actions afterward?"},{"n":12,"title":"How love becomes a life","sanskrit":"भक्तियोग","subtitle":"Love made visible in conduct","mood":"green","scenes":[{"id":"12.1","title":"The path of relationship","ref":2,"range":"12.1–7","source":"12.1–7.","beats":[{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"Which are more firmly established in yoga: those who worship you with devotion, or those who contemplate the imperishable, unmanifest reality?","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Those whose minds rest in me, who worship with steadfast faith, are deeply united in yoga. Those who follow the unmanifest also reach the goal, though that path is harder for embodied beings.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"For those devoted to me, I become the deliverer from the ocean of birth and death.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The chapter recognises more than one approach while emphasising personal devotion. It explains an accessible path rather than requiring everyone to begin with abstraction.","example":"Some people begin with an idea; others through love and relationship. Notice which form of practice allows you to be sincere and steady.","context":"","staging":["Stay close. Cloth, carved wood, Krishna's hand. No new spectacle at the opening of this chapter."]},{"id":"12.2","title":"Begin where you can","ref":9,"range":"12.8–12","source":"12.8–12.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Place your mind and understanding in me. If you cannot yet remain steady, practise returning.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"If that practice is difficult, do your work for me. If you cannot manage even that, give up possessive attachment to the fruits of action and discipline yourself.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"There is peace in that relinquishment.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Krishna offers a progression of accessible practices. The response to difficulty is another honest entry point.","example":"If a long daily practice keeps failing, choose a smaller one you can keep. Consistency can grow from a modest beginning.","context":"","staging":["A short flight of steps appears as a teaching image, then resolves into the chariot step. The reader is not assigned a spiritual rank."]},{"id":"12.3","title":"What devotion looks like","ref":13,"range":"12.13–16","source":"12.13–16.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Dear to me is the one who does not hate, who is friendly and compassionate, free from possessiveness and self-importance, patient and steady.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Such a person does not needlessly disturb the world and is not constantly ruled by its disturbances. Devotion shapes how that person lives among others.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Devotion is more than an emotion or ritual. Compassion, patience and reduced self-centredness are among its signs.","example":"Notice how you behave when someone inconveniences you. That ordinary moment may reveal more about your practice than how you feel during a ceremony.","context":"","staging":["Arjun's gaze returns to people on both sides of the field. They have not become anonymous after the cosmic vision."]},{"id":"12.4","title":"Through praise and blame","ref":18,"range":"12.17–20","source":"12.17–20.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Learn steadiness in honour and dishonour, praise and blame, comfort and pain. Let go of the insistence that everything must favour you.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Those who follow this life-giving teaching with faith, taking me as their highest aim, are deeply dear to me.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Balance does not require ignoring useful criticism. It means responding without letting praise inflate you or blame erase your worth.","example":"Read feedback for what can improve your work. Separate that useful information from the impulse to defend your entire identity.","context":"","staging":["The ordinary body and its shifting sensations become the subject of chapter XIII."]}],"understand":"Devotion includes a relationship with Krishna, a discipline of attention and a way of treating beings. This chapter gives practical entry points without reducing spiritual life to a pleasant feeling.","reflection":"How could care become visible in one ordinary interaction today?"},{"n":13,"title":"The field and its knower","sanskrit":"क्षेत्रक्षेत्रज्ञविभागयोग","subtitle":"Body, awareness and discernment","mood":"blue","scenes":[{"id":"13.1","title":"What is being known?","ref":2,"range":"13.1–7","source":"13.1–7 in this project's edition, which includes Arjun's opening question. See the numbering note before linking other editions.","beats":[{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"Explain nature and spirit, the field and its knower. What is knowledge, and what is to be known?","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"This body is called the field. The one who knows it is the knower of the field. Know me also as the knower in all fields.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Body, senses, mind, desire, aversion, pleasure and pain belong to the changing field. Distinguishing the field and its knower is central to knowledge.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Kshetra means field; kshetrajna means knower of the field. The distinction invites inquiry into experience and awareness, beyond identifying only with the body.","example":"You can notice a sensation, a mood or a thought. Take a moment to distinguish what is being noticed from the fact of noticing.","context":"","staging":["The view opens from Arjun's hand to the changing sensations and thoughts represented around it. The observer is not drawn as a miniature person inside his head."]},{"id":"13.2","title":"How we become able to see","ref":8,"range":"13.8–12","source":"13.8–12.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Humility, sincerity, non-harming, patience, self-restraint, steadiness and care for the teacher belong to knowledge.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"So do reflection on birth, age, illness and death; freedom from possessiveness; devotion; and sustained inquiry into the self. These shape the person who seeks to understand.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The Gita connects knowing with the character of the knower. How you live can support or obstruct discernment.","example":"When trying to understand a disagreement, practise patience and honesty about your own stake in it. Cleverness alone may only strengthen your defence.","context":"","staging":["The shifting images settle enough to be observed. Clarity comes through attention, not the accumulation of labels."]},{"id":"13.3","title":"Near and beyond","ref":17,"range":"13.13–19","source":"13.13–19.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Hear of what is to be known, through knowing which one reaches the deathless: beginningless, supreme Brahman, beyond the usual division of being and non-being.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"It is within and outside beings, moving and unmoving, near and far. Undivided, it appears among divided forms. It is the light of lights, present in the heart.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The text uses paradox to point beyond a simple object you could isolate. It is asking for contemplation, not merely a new label.","example":"When a description seems contradictory, pause before forcing it into a familiar category. Ask whether the category itself may be too narrow.","context":"","staging":["A single light appears through many surfaces without splitting into rival sources. The image is suggestive, not a scientific model."]},{"id":"13.4","title":"Seeing across difference","ref":28,"range":"13.20–35","source":"13.20–35.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Nature brings forth the changing instruments and actions. The conscious principle experiences pleasure and pain; attachment to nature's qualities binds it to embodiment.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Some approach understanding through meditation, some through knowledge, some through action, and others by listening and practising what they hear.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"See the supreme presence equally in beings, enduring amid what perishes. Learn to distinguish the field from its knower, and the way beyond bondage.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The chapter brings discernment back to equal vision. Seeing a shared spiritual presence challenges indifference and contempt.","example":"Before reducing someone to a label or a single action, remember that their inner life is not exhausted by your present view of them.","context":"","staging":[]}],"understand":"Kshetra means field; kshetrajna means its knower. This is a spiritual inquiry into body, experience and awareness. Traditions differ on the precise relationship between the individual knower and the divine; the visual should not quietly decide that debate.","reflection":"Can you notice a thought or feeling without making it the whole account of who you are?"},{"n":14,"title":"The forces within","sanskrit":"गुणत्रयविभागयोग","subtitle":"Clarity, restlessness and inertia","mood":"earth","scenes":[{"id":"14.1","title":"Three qualities","ref":5,"range":"14.1–9","source":"14.1–9.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Nature's three qualities bind the enduring self to embodied life. They are sattva, rajas and tamas.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Sattva brings light and clarity, yet binds through attachment to happiness and knowledge. Rajas brings craving and restless activity, binding through action. Tamas obscures understanding through negligence, inertia and sleep.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The gunas are interacting qualities, not three permanent types of person. Observe patterns without turning them into fixed identities.","example":"After good rest you may think clearly; under pressure you may become restless; when depleted you may withdraw. These observations can help you recognise changing conditions.","context":"","staging":["The same landscape passes through clear light, agitated movement and obscuring haze. No quality is assigned permanently to a character's skin, identity or social group."]},{"id":"14.2","title":"Recognise the movement","ref":11,"range":"14.10–18","source":"14.10–18.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"The qualities rise and fall in relation to one another. Clarity signals sattva; greed and ceaseless striving signal rajas; confusion and neglect signal tamas.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Their influence shapes conduct and its consequences, including the course of further births. Learn to recognise what is becoming strong in you.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Ask what quality is shaping your attention right now. Awareness creates room for a more considered response.","example":"Before making a decision, notice whether you are clear, agitated or too exhausted to think. Where possible, improve the conditions before deciding.","context":"","staging":["The effects overlap. They never become three sealed rooms or fixed personality categories."]},{"id":"14.3","title":"Beyond being carried away","ref":19,"range":"14.19–25","source":"14.19–25.","beats":[{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"How does someone who has gone beyond these qualities behave? How can they cross them?","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Such a person does not hate clarity, activity or confusion when they appear, nor crave them when they depart. They recognise the qualities at work and remain steady.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"They are less bound by pleasure and pain, praise and blame, friendship and hostility, or the need to make every undertaking a possession of the ego.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The aim is not to control every passing mood. It is to loosen the identification that turns a mood into a complete account of yourself.","example":"Instead of “I am a failure,” notice “Discouragement is present.” The feeling still matters, but it no longer has to define the whole person.","context":"","staging":[]},{"id":"14.4","title":"Beyond even clarity","ref":26,"range":"14.26–27","source":"14.26–27.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"One who serves me through unwavering devotion crosses beyond the three qualities and becomes fit for Brahman.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"I am the foundation of the imperishable Brahman, of immortality, enduring dharma and complete happiness.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The Gita’s goal is spiritual freedom, not merely feeling calm. Devotion carries the teaching beyond the management of moods.","example":"Appreciate a peaceful day without demanding that every day feel the same. Let your commitment survive a change in emotional weather.","context":"","staging":["The coloured movements gather into the branches of a tree. The next chapter asks what holds that whole structure in place."]}],"understand":"Gunas are qualities of nature, with ethical, psychological and cosmological roles in the teaching. They are not medical diagnoses or three immutable personality types. The goal is liberation beyond them, not being permanently in a good mood.","reflection":"What is shaping your attention right now: clarity, restless wanting, or difficulty engaging?"},{"n":15,"title":"Roots beyond sight","sanskrit":"पुरुषोत्तमयोग","subtitle":"The tree of life and the highest person","mood":"green","scenes":[{"id":"15.1","title":"The inverted tree","ref":1,"range":"15.1–4","source":"15.1–4.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Think of an enduring tree with roots above and branches below. Its leaves are the Vedic hymns. Its branches are nourished by the qualities of nature; its shoots reach toward the objects of the senses.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Further roots bind beings through action. Its beginning and full extent are not easily seen here. Cut its binding hold with firm non-attachment, and seek the source from which this ancient movement came.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The tree is a spiritual metaphor for conditioned existence. Cutting attachment means loosening bondage, not destroying the natural world or abandoning care.","example":"A craving can grow branches into comparison, spending and anxiety. Trace the pattern back toward its root instead of trimming only one consequence.","context":"","staging":["A vast peepal tree unfolds with roots above and branches below. It surrounds the chariot as a teaching image; its leaves bear light rather than a mass of unreadable text."]},{"id":"15.2","title":"The traveller","ref":7,"range":"15.5–11","source":"15.5–11.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Free from pride and delusion, overcoming possessive attachment, the seeker turns toward the imperishable destination.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"The enduring living self is a portion of me. It draws the mind and senses into embodied life and carries them onward, as wind carries fragrance from its source.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Disciplined discernment sees what an untrained view misses.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Krishna describes the individual self’s relationship to the divine and its passage through embodiment. Traditions interpret this relationship in different ways.","example":"Your habits influence what you notice wherever you go. As a practical analogy, changing the setting does not automatically change the patterns you bring with you.","context":"","staging":["A trace of fragrance passes through the branches. It does not become a literal glowing human soul flying out of a body."]},{"id":"15.3","title":"The support of life","ref":15,"range":"15.12–15","source":"15.12–15.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"The radiance of sun, moon and fire is mine. Entering the earth, I sustain beings. I nourish plants and support the fire by which food is digested.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"I dwell in every heart. Memory, knowledge and their loss arise from me. Through the Vedas, it is I who am to be known.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The teaching directs attention toward the divine support of bodily and mental life. It invites gratitude rather than a purely mechanical view of existence.","example":"Before a meal, pause to appreciate nourishment, digestion and the many supports you did not create for yourself.","context":"","staging":["Light travels from the large tree to a small leaf, then returns us to the living figures on the chariot."]},{"id":"15.4","title":"The highest person","ref":18,"range":"15.16–20","source":"15.16–20.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"There is the perishable and the imperishable. Beyond them is the supreme person, who enters and sustains the worlds.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Because I transcend the perishable and am beyond the imperishable, I am called Purushottama, the highest person. One who knows me in this way worships with their whole being.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Purushottama means the supreme or highest person. The chapter gathers its images into a theological account of Krishna’s ultimate reality.","example":"Some questions ask for a useful technique; others ask what your life is ultimately grounded in. Allow space for the second kind of question as well.","context":"","staging":[]}],"understand":"The tree concerns conditioned existence and bondage, not a command to reject the natural world. The relationship among the individual self, the imperishable and Krishna receives different explanations in different traditions; retain those distinctions in close reading.","reflection":"Which attachment has grown branches through more of your life than you first realised?"},{"n":16,"title":"What we cultivate","sanskrit":"दैवासुरसम्पद्विभागयोग","subtitle":"Qualities that free and qualities that bind","mood":"earth","scenes":[{"id":"16.1","title":"Qualities that free","ref":1,"range":"16.1–5","source":"16.1–5.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Fearlessness, purity of heart, steadiness in knowledge, generosity and self-restraint belong to divine qualities.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"So do truthfulness, non-harming, freedom from anger, compassion, gentleness, modesty and freedom from malice.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"These qualities lead toward freedom. Pride, arrogance, anger, harshness and ignorance bind. Do not despair, Arjun; you are endowed with divine qualities.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Character is a practice. The list gives concrete directions in which your conduct can grow.","example":"Choose one quality for an ordinary day—truthfulness, patience or generosity—and look for a small situation in which you can actually practise it.","context":"","staging":["Our attention returns to human gestures: an open hand, a lowered fist, someone listening. This is a contemplative montage, not invented events within the armies."]},{"id":"16.2","title":"A hunger without an end","ref":12,"range":"16.6–16","source":"16.6–16.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"When people abandon discernment about what should and should not be done, desire can become their only rule.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"They think: I have gained this; I will gain more. This rival is defeated; I will defeat another. They become entangled in anxiety, bound by countless expectations, seeking wealth through unjust means.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The chapter criticises destructive patterns of thought and conduct. It is not an invitation to label groups of people as inherently evil.","example":"Notice when a goal keeps moving the moment you reach it. Ask whether the pursuit has a meaningful purpose or has become a habit of wanting more.","context":"","staging":["A hand closes around more than it can hold. The material falls, but the grip tightens."]},{"id":"16.3","title":"Three gates","ref":21,"range":"16.17–22","source":"16.17–22.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Self-importance can even turn sacrifice into a display. Desire, anger and greed are three gates to ruin. Give them up.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Freed from them, a person can act for their own true good and move toward the highest goal.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Desire here is consuming craving, not every ordinary preference. Watch for impulses that narrow judgment and disregard consequences.","example":"Before sending an angry message, taking an unfair advantage or chasing a risky gain, pause and name which impulse is pressing you.","context":"","staging":["Three narrowing passages are suggested through shadow, not built as a literal new underworld level. The return is to a hand opening."]},{"id":"16.4","title":"A guide beyond impulse","ref":24,"range":"16.23–24","source":"16.23–24.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"One who throws aside the guidance of the teaching and acts only on desire does not reach fulfilment, happiness or the highest goal.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Let the teaching guide your discernment about what should be done and what should be avoided. Having understood it, act.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The Gita asks you to consult shastra—authoritative teaching—rather than treating immediate desire as the final measure.","example":"When a choice is tempting, compare it with the commitments and ethical principles you endorse when thinking clearly. Seek thoughtful guidance if the conflict remains.","context":"","staging":["Arjun raises the question that follows naturally: what about sincere faith that does not follow those prescriptions?"]}],"understand":"The chapter describes qualities and patterns of conduct. Our adaptation must not use its language to label a real-world ethnicity, religion or social group as demonic. Its appeal to shastra, authoritative teaching, is preserved rather than replaced with \"follow whatever feels right.\"","reflection":"Which quality do your repeated actions make stronger?"},{"n":17,"title":"The heart of an offering","sanskrit":"श्रद्धात्रयविभागयोग","subtitle":"Faith, speech, discipline and giving","mood":"gold","scenes":[{"id":"17.1","title":"What shapes faith?","ref":3,"range":"17.1–10","source":"17.1–10.","beats":[{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"What of people who worship with faith but set aside the prescribed teaching? What is the character of their faith?","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Faith reflects the nature of the person and is shaped by the three qualities. What one places faith in helps shape what one becomes.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Even preferences in food reflect these qualities. Severe practices driven by vanity and desire, which torment the body and the divine within it, do not become holy through their severity.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Shraddha is a deep orientation of trust. Examine the qualities your practices are actually cultivating.","example":"Ask whether a routine is making you clearer and kinder, or merely more proud of your endurance. Difficulty alone is not proof of value.","context":"","staging":[]},{"id":"17.2","title":"Discipline in body, speech and mind","ref":15,"range":"17.11–19","source":"17.11–19.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"An offering can be made from duty without craving reward, or from desire for gain and display, or with disregard for care and guidance.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Discipline of the body includes purity, sincerity, restraint and non-harming. Discipline of speech joins truth with what is considerate and beneficial, and with study. Discipline of mind includes calmness, gentleness, self-command and sincerity.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Truth and kindness belong together with usefulness. This does not require avoiding every difficult conversation.","example":"Before giving criticism, ask: is it accurate, useful and expressed with care? You can be direct without adding humiliation.","context":"","staging":["One figure prepares to speak; the gesture slows before the words are released. The scene invites attention to intention without asking the reader to perform a breathing exercise."]},{"id":"17.3","title":"The same gift, a different act","ref":20,"range":"17.20–22","source":"17.20–22.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"A gift given because it should be given, at a fitting time and place, to someone who cannot repay it, is shaped by sattva.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Giving with reluctance or expectation of a return is shaped by rajas. Giving at an unsuitable time or place, without respect or care, is shaped by tamas.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Generosity includes motive, timing and respect. The receiver should not become indebted to your need for admiration.","example":"Help someone in a way they can use. Avoid turning the favour into a future claim on their time, loyalty or praise.","context":"","staging":["Three brief images show an offering given respectfully, given while waiting for repayment, and tossed carelessly toward its recipient. No version uses a humiliating depiction of poverty."]},{"id":"17.4","title":"Om, Tat, Sat","ref":23,"range":"17.23–28","source":"17.23–28.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Om, Tat, Sat: these sacred designations of Brahman accompany sacrifice, giving and discipline.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Tat turns the act beyond a personal claim on its reward. Sat is associated with being, goodness and steadiness in worthy action. An offering empty of faith lacks the spiritual foundation it appears to claim.","bridge":false}],"simple":"A sacred phrase is not a substitute for sincerity. The chapter closes by returning from outward performance to the inner orientation of the act.","example":"Before doing something meaningful, take a moment to remember its purpose. A clear intention can keep the task from becoming mere performance.","context":"","staging":["The three words may appear once in Sanskrit with a short English explanation. They are not buttons that trigger a magical reward."]}],"understand":"Shraddha is faith or deeply held trust expressed through a life. The chapter examines food, worship, discipline and giving through the gunas; the food categories are not presented as modern nutritional advice.","reflection":"What changes in a gift when you stop keeping an invisible account of what should come back?"},{"n":18,"title":"The choice remains","sanskrit":"मोक्षसंन्यासयोग","subtitle":"Responsibility, surrender and clarity","mood":"dawn","scenes":[{"id":"18.1","title":"What must be relinquished?","ref":9,"range":"18.1–18","source":"18.1–18.","beats":[{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"Tell me clearly the difference between renunciation and relinquishment.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Renunciation is understood as leaving actions driven by desire. Relinquishment is giving up attachment to the fruits of action.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Sacrifice, giving and discipline should not simply be abandoned. Perform them without possessiveness or the demand for reward. Leaving a duty only because it is difficult is not the highest relinquishment.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"An action has several conditions: its setting, the agent, the instruments, the different efforts and the divine factor. To see the isolated self as the sole cause is to see incompletely.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Tyaga means relinquishment. Krishna distinguishes giving up attachment from simply refusing necessary action.","example":"Do the repair or apology that is yours to make. You cannot control whether it earns praise or immediate forgiveness, but that uncertainty need not prevent it.","context":"","staging":["The original battlefield begins to return around the teaching landscape."]},{"id":"18.2","title":"Discern the work that is yours","ref":47,"range":"18.19–48","source":"18.19–48.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Knowledge can recognise one enduring reality across many beings, or see only their separateness, or cling to a fragment as though it were the whole.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Action, the one who acts, understanding, determination and happiness are also shaped by the qualities. What seems pleasing at first may bind later; what asks effort at first may lead toward clarity.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Sanjaya","text":"Krishna speaks within the social order of the epic, describing duties of its four varnas. He then returns Arjun to the responsibility that is his to meet.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Through one's own work, one can worship the source from which beings arise. Better one's own duty, imperfectly carried out, than another's performed well. Every undertaking has some fault, as fire is accompanied by smoke.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The text discusses work within its historical social framework. This telling emphasises discernment of responsibility and aptitude; it does not turn social status into a measure of human worth.","example":"Learn from others without copying their whole life. Ask which responsibilities and abilities are actually yours, and accept that meaningful work still involves difficulty.","context":"Verses 18.41–48 concern varna and duty within the text's social framework. This is not simply a modern instruction to \"follow your passion.\" See the context note on social hierarchy and svadharma.","staging":[]},{"id":"18.3","title":"Consider what you have heard","ref":63,"range":"18.49–63","source":"18.49–63.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"With purified understanding, restraint, freedom from pride and possessiveness, and a heart no longer ruled by craving, the seeker becomes ready for Brahman. Through devotion, I am known in truth.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Offer your actions to me. Take refuge in me. If pride makes you refuse to listen, it will lead you astray; your own nature will still press upon you.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"I have opened this teaching to you. Consider it thoroughly. Then act as you choose.","bridge":false}],"simple":"Krishna explicitly asks Arjun to reflect and choose. Guidance should deepen discernment rather than replace it with unthinking obedience.","example":"When advice matters, examine it carefully and understand its reasons. Then accept responsibility for the decision you make.","context":"","staging":["Krishna and Arjun are once again at the ordinary scale of two figures speaking. The armies are fully visible. There is no animated \"correct answer\" waiting above Arjun.","Leave space after the line. Do not end the chapter here; Krishna speaks again."]},{"id":"18.4","title":"I am ready","ref":73,"range":"18.64–78","source":"18.64–78. Lifting the bow is authored visual staging of Arjun's resolve in 18.73, not an additional quoted event.","beats":[{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"You are dear to me, so hear my deepest word again. Let your mind rest in me. Be devoted to me, worship and bow to me. You will come to me; I promise you this because you are dear to me.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Relinquish all dharmas and take refuge in me alone. I will free you from all sin. Do not grieve.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Krishna","text":"Have you listened with attention, Arjun? Has the confusion born of ignorance been dispelled?","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Arjun","text":"Through your grace, my confusion has cleared. I have recovered my understanding. I stand steady, free of doubt. I will act according to your word.","bridge":false},{"speaker":"Sanjaya","text":"That is the conversation I heard, a wonder that still moves me as I remember it. Where Krishna, master of yoga, and Arjun, the archer, stand together, I see fortune, victory, flourishing and steadfast right conduct.","bridge":false}],"simple":"The ending joins devotion, clarity and responsible action. The journey does not promise a life without difficulty; it shows a transformed relationship to it.","example":"Carry one teaching into the next real decision you face. Let the insight become a considered action, however small.","context":"","staging":["No burst of light. Krishna's familiar hand rests near the reins. The word about surrender is allowed its weight.","Arjun reaches for the bow. Match the opening composition: the same hand, now steady. The people across the field remain recognisable. Readiness does not erase the cost of what follows."]}],"understand":"The conclusion holds together discernment, action, devotion and surrender. The invitation to choose at 18.63 is followed by Krishna's further teaching and Arjun's response. Interpretations of \"relinquish all dharmas\" in 18.66 differ; do not reduce it to either casual rule-breaking or an instruction to abandon care for others.","reflection":"What would it mean to act with greater clarity in the life before you?"}]};
